WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.300
The OpenAI Agents SDK.

2
00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:07.616
It is OpenAI's own opinion on how simple agent code should be.

3
00:00:07.616 --> 00:00:09.300
Just five building blocks.

4
00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:12.067
Here are the five primitives.

5
00:00:12.367 --> 00:00:14.967
Agents: a model, instructions, and tools.

6
00:00:15.267 --> 00:00:19.200
Handoffs: one agent transfers the whole conversation to another.

7
00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:23.767
Guardrails: validators that can halt a run before tokens are wasted.

8
00:00:24.067 --> 00:00:25.367
Sessions give memory.

9
00:00:25.667 --> 00:00:27.900
And tracing is built in for every run.

10
00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:30.533
Here's a typical setup.

11
00:00:30.833 --> 00:00:34.767
A guardrail gates the door, and rejects off-topic requests early.

12
00:00:35.067 --> 00:00:38.367
A triage agent decides who should handle the request.

13
00:00:38.667 --> 00:00:42.833
Then it hands off the entire conversation to a specialist, who answers.

14
00:00:44.033 --> 00:00:45.767
Here it is in code, shortened.

15
00:00:46.067 --> 00:00:50.333
We define two specialists, one for asteroids and one for space weather.

16
00:00:50.633 --> 00:00:55.067
The triage agent lists them as handoffs, and attaches an input guardrail.

17
00:00:55.367 --> 00:00:57.433
Then the runner executes the request.

18
00:00:58.633 --> 00:00:59.800
Now a real use.

19
00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:01.646
You port your own router.

20
00:01:01.646 --> 00:01:05.233
Triage hands off to asteroid or space weather specialists.

21
00:01:05.533 --> 00:01:11.667
The on topic guardrail rejects a request like write my homework before a specialist ever sees it.

22
00:01:11.967 --> 00:01:17.767
And the trace viewer shows the handoff chain per request, observability you'd otherwise build yourself.

23
00:01:18.967 --> 00:01:19.816
One caution.

24
00:01:19.816 --> 00:01:27.533
The SDK is opinionated about staying inside its ecosystem, and relying on it blindly hides how routing works.

25
00:01:27.833 --> 00:01:31.631
The fix: keep both versions in your repo, raw and SDK.

26
00:01:31.631 --> 00:01:35.500
That's a portfolio piece that shows real understanding.

27
00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:37.534
Let's recap.

28
00:01:37.534 --> 00:01:43.167
The SDK has five primitives: agents, handoffs, guardrails, sessions, and tracing.

29
00:01:43.467 --> 00:01:45.933
A handoff transfers the whole conversation.

30
00:01:46.233 --> 00:01:48.067
Guardrails stop bad runs early.

31
00:01:48.367 --> 00:01:51.666
It's convenient, but tied to its ecosystem.

32
00:01:51.666 --> 00:01:53.200
Next up: PydanticAI.
